"CSS Values and Units Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/)\nciteturn1609view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/\",\"lineno\":520}); Total lines: 2959\nL501: To fetch a style resource from a 【222†url†url.spec.whatwg.org】 or 【183†<url>】 `urlValue`, given an 【249†CSS rule】 or a 【250†css declaration block】 `cssRuleOrDeclaration`, a string `destination` matching a `【259†RequestDestination†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】`, a \"no-cors\" or \"cors\" `corsMode`, and an algorithm `processResponse` accepting a 【260†response†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 and a null, failure or byte stream:\nL502:   1. Let `parsedUrl` be the result of 【234†resolving】 `urlValue` given `cssRuleOrDeclaration`. If that failed, return.\nL503: \nL504:   2. Let `settingsObject` be `cssRuleOrDeclaration`’s 【255†relevant settings object†html.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL505:   3.\nL506: Let `req` be a new 【261†request†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 whose 【262†url†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is `parsedUrl`, whose 【263†destination†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is `destination`, 【264†mode†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is `corsMode`, 【265†origin†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is `settingsObject`’s 【266†origin†html.spec.whatwg.org】, 【267†credentials mode†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is \"same-origin\", 【268†use-url-credentials flag†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is set, 【269†client†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is `settingsObject`, and whose 【270†referrer†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is \"client\".\nL507:   4. If `corsMode` is \"no-cors\", set `req`’s 【267†credentials mode†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 to \"include\".\nL508: \nL509:   5. Apply any URL request modifier steps that apply to this request.\nL510: \nL511: Note: This specification does not define any URL request modification steps, but other specs may do so.\nL512: \nL513:   6. If `req`’s 【264†mode†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 is \"cors\", and `sheet` is not null, then set `req`’s 【270†referrer†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 to the 【257†style resource base URL】 given `cssRuleOrDeclaration`. 【271†[CSSOM]】\nL514:   7. If `sheet`’s 【272†origin-clean flag】 is set, set `req`’s 【273†initiator type†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 to \"css\". 【271†[CSSOM]】\nL515: \nL516:   8. 【274†Fetch†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 `req`, with 【275†processResponseConsumeBody†fetch.spec.whatwg.org】 set to `processResponse`.\nL517: \nL518: When interpreting 【222†URLs†url.spec.whatwg.org】 expressed in CSS, the 【258†URL parser’s†url.spec.whatwg.org】 `encoding` argument must be omitted (i.e. use the default, UTF-8), regardless of the stylesheet encoding.\nL519: Note: In other words, a URL written in CSS will always 【276†percent-encode†url.spec.whatwg.org】 non-ASCII codepoints using UTF-8 in the 【222†URL†url.spec.whatwg.org】 object (and thus whenever using the URL value for e.g. network requests), regardless of the stylesheet’s own encoding. Note that this occurs 【277†after decoding the stylesheet】 into Unicode 【278†code points†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL520: ## 5. Numeric Data Types\nL521: \nL522: Numeric data types are used to represent quantities, indexes, positions, and other such values. Although many syntactic variations can exist in expressing the quantity (numeric aspect) in a given numeric value, the 【184†specified】 and 【175†computed value】 do not distinguish these variations: they represent the value’s abstract quantity, not its syntactic representation.\nL523: The numeric data types include 【147†<integer>】, 【279†<number>】, 【140†<percentage>】, and various 【280†dimensions】 including 【127†<length>】, 【281†<angle>】, 【282†<time>】, 【283†<frequency>】, and 【284†<resolution>】.\nL524: \nL525: Note: While general-purpose 【280†dimensions】 are defined here, some other modules define additional data types (e.g. 【285†[css-grid-1]】 introduces 【286†fr】 units) whose usage is more localized.\nL526: The precision and supported range of numeric values in CSS is 【287†implementation-defined†infra.spec.whatwg.org】, and can vary based on the property or other context a value is used in. However, within the CSS specifications, infinite precision and range is assumed. When a value cannot be explicitly supported due to range/precision limitations, it must be converted to the closest value supported by the implementation, but how the implementation defines \"closest\" is implementation-defined as well.\nL527: If an 【281†<angle>】 must be converted due to exceeding the implementation-defined range of supported values, it must be clamped to the nearest supported multiple of 360deg.\nL528: ### 5.1. Range Restrictions and Range Definition Notation\nL529: Properties can restrict numeric values to some range. If the value is outside the allowed range, then unless otherwise specified, the declaration is invalid and must be 【288†ignored†www.w3.org】. Range restrictions can be annotated in the numeric type notation using CSS bracketed range notation—​`[`min`,`max`]`—​within the angle brackets, after the identifying keyword, indicating a closed range between (and including) `min` and `max`.\nL530: For example, 【147†<integer [0,10]>】 indicates an integer between 0 and 10, inclusive, while 【281†<angle [0,180deg]>】 indicates an angle between 0deg and 180deg (expressed in any unit).\nL531: Note: CSS values generally do not allow open ranges; thus only square-bracket notation is used.\nL532: \nL533: CSS theoretically supports infinite precision and infinite ranges for all value types; however in reality implementations have finite capacity. 【158†UAs】 should support reasonably useful ranges and precisions. Range extremes that are ideally unlimited are indicated using ∞ or −∞ as appropriate. For example, 【127†<length [0,∞]>】 indicates a non-negative length.\nL534: If no range is indicated, either by using the 【289†bracketed range notation】 or in the property description, then `[−∞,∞]` is assumed.\nL535: \nL536: Values of −∞ or ∞ must be written without units, even if the value type uses units. Values of 0 can be written without units, even if the value type doesn’t allow “unitless zeroes” (such as 【282†<time>】).\nL537: Note: At the time of writing, the 【289†bracketed range notation】 is new; thus in most CSS specifications any range limitations are described only in prose. (For example, “Negative values are not allowed” or “Negative values are invalid” indicate a `[0,∞]` range.) This does not make them any less binding.\nL538: ### 5.2. Integers: the 【147†<integer>】 type\nL539: \nL540: Integer values are denoted by <integer>.\nL541: \nL542: When written literally, an integer is one or more decimal digits 0 through 9 and corresponds to a subset of the 【290†<number-token>】 production in the CSS Syntax Module 【187†[CSS-SYNTAX-3]】. The first digit of an integer may be immediately preceded by - or + to indicate the integer’s sign.\nL543: \nL544: Tests\nL545:   * 【291†multicol-count-non-integer-001.xht†wpt.fyi】 【292†(live test)†wpt.live】 【293†(source)†github.com】\nL546:   * 【294†multicol-count-non-integer-002.xht†wpt.fyi】 【295†(live test)†wpt.live】 【296†(source)†github.com】\nL547:   * 【297†multicol-count-non-integer-003.xht†wpt.fyi】 【298†(live test)†wpt.live】 【299†(source)†github.com】\nL548:   * 【300†numbers-units-001.xht†wpt.fyi】 【301†(live test)†wpt.live】 【302†(source)†github.com】\nL549:   * 【303†numbers-units-004.xht†wpt.fyi】 【304†(live test)†wpt.live】 【305†(source)†github.com】\nL550: Unless otherwise specified, in the CSS specifications rounding to the nearest integer requires rounding in the direction of +∞ when the fractional portion is exactly 0.5. (For example, 1.5 rounds to 2, while -1.5 rounds to -1.)\nL551: #### 5.2.1. Computation and Combination of 【147†<integer>】\nL552: \nL553: Unless otherwise specified, the 【175†computed value】 of a specified 【147†<integer>】 is the specified abstract integer.\nL554: \nL555: 【178†Interpolation】 of 【147†<integer>】 is defined as `V`_{result} = round((1 - `p`) × `V_{A}` + `p` × `V_{B}`); that is, interpolation happens in the real number space as for 【279†<number>】s, and the result is converted to an <integer> by 【306†rounding to the nearest integer】.\nL556: 【177†Addition】 of 【147†<integer>】 is defined as `V_{result}` = `V_{A}` + `V_{B}`\nL557: \nL558: Tests\nL559: \nL560:   * 【307†calc-positive-fraction-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【308†(live test)†wpt.live】 【309†(source)†github.com】\nL561:   * 【310†rgba-011.html†wpt.fyi】 【311†(live test)†wpt.live】 【312†(source)†github.com】\nL562: ### 5.3. Real Numbers: the 【279†<number>】 type\nL563: \nL564: Number values are denoted by <number>, and represent real numbers, possibly with a fractional component.\nL565: \nL566: Tests\nL567: \nL568:   * 【313†animation-iteration-count-calc.html†wpt.fyi】 【314†(live test)†wpt.live】 【315†(source)†github.com】\nL569:   * numbers-units-002.xht (visual test) 【316†(source)†github.com】\nL570:   * 【317†numbers-units-003.xht†wpt.fyi】 【318†(live test)†wpt.live】 【319†(source)†github.com】\nL571: When written literally, a number is either an 【320†integer】, or zero or more decimal digits followed by a dot (.) followed by one or more decimal digits; optionally, it can be concluded by the letter “e” or “E” followed by an integer indicating the base-ten exponent in 【321†scientific notation†en.wikipedia.org】. It corresponds to the 【290†<number-token>】 production in the 【322†CSS Syntax Module†www.w3.org】 【187†[CSS-SYNTAX-3]】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Flexible Box Layout Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-flexbox-1/)\nciteturn1609view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-flexbox-1/\",\"lineno\":2227}); Total lines: 3589\nL2197: When determining the 【2359†flex base size】, the item’s min and max 【162†main sizes】 are ignored (no clamping occurs). Furthermore, the sizing calculations that floor the content box size at zero when applying 【2508†box-sizing】 are also ignored. (For example, an item with a specified size of zero, positive padding, and box-sizing: border-box will have an outer flex base size of zero—​and hence a negative inner flex base size.)\nL2198: The 【3620†hypothetical main size】 is the item’s 【2359†flex base size】 clamped according to its 【3588†used】 min and max 【162†main sizes】 (and flooring the content box size at zero).\nL2199: \nL2200: Tests\nL2201:      * 【3621†text-as-flexitem-size-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【3622†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3623†(source)†github.com】\nL2202:   3. Determine the 【162†main size】 of the flex container using the rules of the formatting context in which it participates.\nL2203: The 【3624†automatic block size】 of a 【179†block-level】 【151†flex container】 is its 【2486†max-content size】.\nL2204: \nL2205: The Block Layout spec should define this equivalency, but it doesn’t exist yet.\nL2206: ### 9.3. Main Size Determination\nL2207:   1. Collect flex items into flex lines:\nL2208:      * If the flex container is 【1796†single-line】, collect all the flex items into a single flex line.\nL2209:      * Otherwise, starting from the first uncollected item, collect consecutive items one by one until the first time that the next collected item would not fit into the flex container’s 【3625†inner】 main size (or until a forced break is encountered, see 【69†§ 10 Fragmenting Flex Layout】). If the very first uncollected item wouldn’t fit, collect just it into the line.\nL2210: For this step, the size of a flex item is its 【3626†outer】 【3620†hypothetical main size】. (Note: This can be negative.)\nL2211: \nL2212: Repeat until all flex items have been collected into flex lines.\nL2213: \nL2214: Note that the \"collect as many\" line will collect zero-sized flex items onto the end of the previous line even if the last non-zero item exactly \"filled up\" the line.\nL2215: \nL2216:   2. 【60†Resolve the flexible lengths】 of all the flex items to find their used 【162†main size】. See 【60†§ 9.7 Resolving Flexible Lengths】.\nL2217: Tests\nL2218:   * 【3627†flexbox_quirks_body.html†wpt.fyi】 【3628†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3629†(source)†github.com】\nL2219:   * 【3630†ortho-table-item-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【3631†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3632†(source)†github.com】\nL2220:   * 【3633†percentage-heights-000.html†wpt.fyi】 【3634†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3635†(source)†github.com】\nL2221:   * 【3636†percentage-heights-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【3637†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3638†(source)†github.com】\nL2222:   * 【3639†percentage-heights-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【3640†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3641†(source)†github.com】\nL2223:   * 【3642†percentage-heights-003.html†wpt.fyi】 【3643†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3644†(source)†github.com】\nL2224:   * 【3645†percentage-heights-004.html†wpt.fyi】 【3646†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3647†(source)†github.com】\nL2225:   * 【3648†percentage-heights-005.html†wpt.fyi】 【3649†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3650†(source)†github.com】\nL2226:   * 【3651†percentage-heights-006.html†wpt.fyi】 【3652†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3653†(source)†github.com】\nL2227:   * 【3654†percentage-heights-007.html†wpt.fyi】 【3655†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3656†(source)†github.com】\nL2228:   * 【3657†percentage-heights-008.html†wpt.fyi】 【3658†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3659†(source)†github.com】\nL2229:   * 【3660†percentage-heights-009.html†wpt.fyi】 【3661†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3662†(source)†github.com】\nL2230:   * 【3663†percentage-heights-010.html†wpt.fyi】 【3664†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3665†(source)†github.com】\nL2231:   * 【3666†percentage-heights-011.html†wpt.fyi】 【3667†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3668†(source)†github.com】\nL2232:   * 【3669†percentage-heights-012.html†wpt.fyi】 【3670†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3671†(source)†github.com】\nL2233:   * 【3672†percentage-heights-013.html†wpt.fyi】 【3673†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3674†(source)†github.com】\nL2234:   * 【3675†percentage-heights-014.html†wpt.fyi】 【3676†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3677†(source)†github.com】\nL2235:   * 【3678†percentage-heights-015.html†wpt.fyi】 【3679†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3680†(source)†github.com】\nL2236:   * 【3681†percentage-heights-016.html†wpt.fyi】 【3682†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3683†(source)†github.com】\nL2237:   * 【3684†percentage-heights-017.html†wpt.fyi】 【3685†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3686†(source)†github.com】\nL2238:   * 【3687†percentage-heights-018.html†wpt.fyi】 【3688†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3689†(source)†github.com】\nL2239:   * 【3690†percentage-heights-019.html†wpt.fyi】 【3691†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3692†(source)†github.com】\nL2240:   * 【3693†percentage-heights-020.html†wpt.fyi】 【3694†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3695†(source)†github.com】\nL2241:   * 【3696†percentage-heights-021.html†wpt.fyi】 【3697†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3698†(source)†github.com】\nL2242:   * 【3699†percentage-heights-022.html†wpt.fyi】 【3700†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3701†(source)†github.com】\nL2243:   * 【3702†percentage-heights-023.html†wpt.fyi】 【3703†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3704†(source)†github.com】\nL2244: ### 9.4. Cross Size Determination\nL2245:   1. Determine the hypothetical cross size of each item by performing layout as if it were an 【144†in-flow】 【3705†block-level box】 with the used 【162†main size】 and the given available space, treating 【1179†auto】 as 【3592†fit-content】. Tests\nL2246:      * 【3706†canvas-contain-size.html†wpt.fyi】 【3707†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3708†(source)†github.com】\nL2247:      * 【3709†inline-flex-column-image-load.html†wpt.fyi】 【3710†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3711†(source)†github.com】\nL2248:   2. Calculate the cross size of each flex line.\nL2249: If the flex container is 【1796†single-line】 and has a 【1190†definite】 【116†cross size】, the cross size of the 【167†flex line】 is the 【151†flex container】’s inner cross size.\nL2250: \nL2251: Otherwise, for each flex line:\nL2252:     1. Collect all the flex items whose inline-axis is parallel to the main-axis, whose 【118†align-self】 is 【3712†baseline】, and whose cross-axis margins are both non-auto. Find the largest of the distances between each item’s baseline and its hypothetical outer cross-start edge, and the largest of the distances between each item’s baseline and its hypothetical outer cross-end edge, and sum these two values.\nL2253:     2. Among all the items not collected by the previous step, find the largest outer 【3713†hypothetical cross size】.\nL2254:     3. The used 【116†cross size】 of the 【167†flex line】 is the largest of the numbers found in the previous two steps and zero.\nL2255: If the flex container is 【1796†single-line】, then clamp the line’s cross-size to be within the container’s computed min and max cross sizes. Note that if CSS 2.1’s definition of min/max-width/height applied more generally, this behavior would fall out automatically.\nL2256: Tests\nL2257:      * 【3714†flexbox-single-line-clamp-1.html†wpt.fyi】 【3715†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3716†(source)†github.com】\nL2258:      * 【3717†flexbox-single-line-clamp-2.html†wpt.fyi】 【3718†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3719†(source)†github.com】\nL2259:      * 【3720†flexbox-single-line-clamp-3.html†wpt.fyi】 【3721†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3722†(source)†github.com】\nL2260:      * 【3723†flex-item-contains-size-layout-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【3724†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3725†(source)†github.com】\nL2261:      * 【3726†flex-item-contains-strict.html†wpt.fyi】 【3727†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3728†(source)†github.com】\nL2262:      * 【3729†layout-algorithm_algo-cross-line-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【3730†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3731†(source)†github.com】\nL2263:      * 【3732†layout-algorithm_algo-cross-line-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【3733†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3734†(source)†github.com】\nL2264:   3. Handle 'align-content: stretch'. If the flex container has a 【1190†definite】 【116†cross size】, 【128†align-content】 is 【3735†stretch】, and the sum of the flex lines' cross sizes is less than the flex container’s inner cross size, increase the cross size of each flex line by equal amounts such that the sum of their cross sizes exactly equals the flex container’s inner cross size.\nL2265:   4. Collapse visibility:collapse items. If any flex items have 【1173†visibility: collapse】, note the 【116†cross size】 of the line they’re in as the item’s `strut size`, and restart layout from the beginning.\nL2266: In this second layout round, when 【3736†collecting items into lines】, treat the collapsed items as having zero 【162†main size】. For the rest of the algorithm following that step, ignore the collapsed items entirely (as if they were display:none) except that after 【3737†calculating the cross size of the lines】, if any line’s cross size is less than the largest `strut size` among all the collapsed items in the line, set its cross size to that `strut size`.\nL2267: \nL2268: Skip this step in the second layout round.\nL2269: Tests\nL2270:      * 【3738†flexbox-collapsed-item-baseline-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【3739†(live test)†wpt.live】 【3740†(source)†github.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Box Alignment Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-align-3/)\nciteturn1609view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-align-3/\",\"lineno\":725}); Total lines: 1179\nL700:   2. Otherwise, if any cells in the row participate in last 【183†baseline alignment】 along the table’s 【110†inline axis】, 【322†generate】 from that 【181†alignment baseline】.\nL701: \nL702:   3. Otherwise, if the row is not empty, 【334†synthesize】 from the lowest and highest 【335†content edges】 of all the cells in the row. See 【99†CSS 2 § 17.5.3 Table height algorithms†www.w3.org】.\nL703: \nL704:   4. Otherwise, use the 【332†block-start】 【335†content edge】 of the table row box itself as the 【181†alignment baseline】.\nL705: For this purpose, any table cell that spans multiple rows is ignored if it’s span does not start in this row; except that for step 2, it’s ignored if its span does not end in this row.\nL706: \nL707: Last baselines are analogous (with “first”/“last” and “start”/“end” inverted).\nL708: \nL709: Spanning cells participate only in the first/last row that they span for the purpose of 【178†first baseline】/【179†last baseline】.\nL710: flex containers\nL711:     See 【336†Flex Baselines†www.w3.org】 in 【101†[CSS-FLEXBOX-1]】.\nL712: grid containers\nL713:     See 【337†Grid Baselines†www.w3.org】 in 【100†[CSS-GRID-1]】.\nL714: \nL715: To generate baselines for a box from a single baseline, use the baseline table from the font settings and 【333†first available font】 of that box, and align that baseline set to the given single baseline.\nL716: If a box that participates in 【183†baseline alignment】 has no 【319†baseline set】, then its 【181†alignment baseline】 is 【334†synthesized】 according to the rules of the 【261†formatting context】 in which it participates. To synthesize baselines from a rectangle (or two parallel lines), synthesize the alphabetic baseline from the 【338†line-under】 line, and the central baseline by averaging the positions of the two edges or lines.\nL717: See 【339†CSS Inline Layout 3 § A Synthesizing Alignment Metrics】 for rules on synthesizing additional baselines.\nL718: Note: The edges used to 【334†synthesize】 baselines from a box depend on their 【261†formatting context】: inline-level boxes synthesize from their margin edges 【15†[CSS-INLINE-3]】, table cells synthesize from their content edges 【95†[CSS2]】, and grid and flex items synthesize from their border edges 【100†[CSS-GRID-1]】 【101†[CSS-FLEXBOX-1]】.\nL719: In general, the 【127†writing mode】 of the box, shape, or other object being aligned is used to determine the 【338†line-under】 and 【340†line-over】 edges for synthesis. However, when that writing mode’s 【341†block flow direction】 is parallel to the axis of the 【130†alignment context】, an axis-compatible writing mode must be assumed:\nL720: \nL721:   * If the box establishing the 【130†alignment context】 has a 【341†block flow direction】 that is orthogonal to the axis of the alignment context, use its 【127†writing mode】.\nL722:   * Otherwise:\nL723: \nL724:     * If the box’s own 【127†writing mode】 is vertical, assume 【342†horizontal-tb】.\nL725: \nL726:     * If the box’s own 【127†writing mode】 is horizontal, assume 【343†vertical-lr】 if 【306†direction】 is 【308†ltr】 and 【344†vertical-rl】 if direction is 【309†rtl】.\nL727: For the purposes of finding the baselines of a box, it and all its 【256†in-flow】 descendants that are 【237†block-axis scroll containers】 must be considered as if scrolled to their initial scroll position. Additionally, if the position of a 【219†scroll container】’s first/last baseline is outside its border edge, that baseline’s position is clamped to the border edge.\nL728: ### 9.2. Baseline Alignment Grouping\nL729: \nL730: A baseline-sharing group is composed of boxes that participate in baseline alignment together. This is possible only if they both:\nL731:   * Share an 【130†alignment context】 along an axis perpendicular to the axis they’re being baseline-aligned in. (For example, grid items with 【107†align-self: baseline】 are baseline-aligning along the grid’s block axis, and therefore participate with other items in their row.)\nL732:   * Have 【345†compatible baseline alignment preferences】 (i.e., the baselines that want to align are on the same side of the alignment context).\nL733: Note: Boxes participating in 【192†baseline content-alignment】 and boxes participating in 【193†baseline self-alignment】 can be part of the same 【182†baseline-sharing group】, and can thus align to each other. The difference between the two methods is in where extra space is inserted to perform the alignment (inside or outside the box’s own border).\nL734: \nL735: Boxes share an alignment context, along a particular axis, and established by a particular box, when they are:\nL736:   * table cells in the same row, along the table’s row (inline) axis, established by the row box\nL737:   * grid items in the same row, along the grid’s row (inline) axis, established by the 【118†grid container】\nL738:   * grid items in the same column, along the grid’s column (block) axis, established by the 【118†grid container】\nL739:   * flex items in the same flex line, along the flex container’s main axis, established by the 【117†flex container】\nL740: Note: Conceptually, the inline-level boxes in a line box also share a self-alignment context and participate in a baseline-sharing group; however they only baseline-align in response to the 【98†vertical-align】 property, not any of the properties defined in this module. See 【15†[CSS-INLINE-3]】.\nL741: If a box spans multiple 【130†shared alignment contexts】, then it participates in first/last baseline alignment within its start-most/end-most shared alignment context along that axis. For example, a table cell spanning three rows participates in 【346†first-baseline alignment】 with the table cells in the first row that it spans, or alternatively in 【347†last-baseline alignment】 with the table cells in the last row that it spans.\nL742: The 【196†baseline alignment preferences】 of two boxes in a 【182†baseline-sharing group】 are compatible if they have:\nL743: \nL744:   * the same 【341†block flow direction】 and same 【196†baseline alignment preference】\nL745: \nL746:   * opposite 【341†block flow direction】 and opposite 【196†baseline alignment preference】\nL747: ### 9.3. Aligning Boxes by Baseline\nL748: \nL749: Given a set of 【126†alignment subjects】 and their baselines that all belong to a single 【182†baseline-sharing group】, the alignment subjects are baseline-aligned as follows:\nL750:   1. Generate the 【182†baseline-sharing group】’s baseline table from the 【333†first available font】 of the group’s 【130†alignment context】 and overlay also the mirror of this baseline table by aligning their central baselines. These are the baseline “grids” to which the 【126†alignment subjects】 will align.\nL751:   2. Align each 【126†alignment subject】 by its specified 【181†alignment baseline】 to the group’s baseline table or its mirror, whichever matches the alignment subject’s 【348†line orientation】. Unless otherwise specified (e.g. via the 【321†alignment-baseline】 property), the alignment baseline is the 【349†dominant baseline】 of the 【130†alignment context】.\nL752:   3. Position the aligned 【182†baseline-sharing group】 within the 【128†alignment container】 according to its 【184†fallback alignment】. The fallback alignment of a baseline-sharing group is the fallback alignment of its items as resolved to 【350†physical directions】.\nL753:   4. For first/last 【192†baseline content-alignment】, then add the minimum necessary extra space between the 【128†alignment container’s】 start/end content edge and the 【126†alignment subject’s】 edge to align the start/end margin edges of all the alignment containers in the 【130†alignment context】 while maintaining baseline alignment within the 【182†baseline-sharing group】.\nL754: ## Appendix A: Static Position Terminology\nL755: When both 【290†inset properties】 in a given axis are 【25†auto】 on an 【351†absolutely positioned box†www.w3.org】, CSS2 uses its 【140†static position】 to resolve its size and position. See 【297†CSS2.1§10.3.7†www.w3.org】. The 【24†box alignment properties】 modify these calculations, just as they do the sizing and positioning calculations in other layout modes.\nL756: These modifications refer to a static-position rectangle, whose edges represent the static position of the box from each side of its 【269†containing block】.\nL757: The 【295†static-position rectangle】 and the 【140†static positions】 to which it corresponds are defined by the layout model of its “hypothetical box”:\nL758: Block Layout\nL759:     The 【140†static positions】 of a 【286†block-level box】 are defined in 【95†[CSS2]】 Chapter 10. The 【295†static-position rectangle】 is a zero-thickness rectangle spanning between the inline-axis sides of the box’s 【282†static-position containing block】 (see 【297†CSS2§10.3.7†www.w3.org】); and positioned at its 【332†block-start】 static position (see 【303†CSS2§10.6.4†www.w3.org】).\nL760: Inline Layout\n"
